    <body>
      <sec id="article-26710.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>No molecule in the periodic table has played a more significant role in the evolution and propagation of life on Earth than oxygen (O). Whether combined with hydrogen (H) to form water as H<sub>2</sub>O or when attached to another oxygen molecule as diatomic (O<sub>2</sub>)/molecular oxygen, this fundamental component of the atmosphere is essential for life. The 2 identical oxygen molecules, chemically linked by a dynamic double bond, serve as the fuel for aerobic metabolism in all mammals and other organisms. This diatomic structure provides oxygen with the inherent stability necessary for cellular and membrane transport, yet offers tremendous chemical versatility to accept and transfer electrons within the mitochondrial apparatus. Oxygen, which makes up&#x000a0;approximately 21% of the&#x000a0;Earth's atmospheric gases by&#x000a0;volume, therefore, evolved to become the&#x000a0;primary oxidizing agent responsible for energy production in nature.&#x000a0;As a consequence of Dalton's Law of&#x000a0;Partial Pressures,&#x000a0;each gas within a confined system exerts&#x000a0;its own&#x000a0;independent pressure. Hence, the total pressure is the sum of&#x000a0;the individual gases' partial pressures within the entirety of the system.</p>
        <p>Atmospheric air is&#x000a0;composed of&#x000a0;approximately 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, and trace amounts&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;other less essential gases, for example, carbon dioxide.&#x000a0;At&#x000a0;sea level, where atmospheric pressure is&#x000a0;760 mmHg, the&#x000a0;estimated&#x000a0;partial pressures of&#x000a0;these&#x000a0;gases&#x000a0;are&#x000a0;approximately&#x000a0;593 mmHg for nitrogen&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;160 mmHg for oxygen. Although nitrogen (N<sub>2</sub>) is considered physiologically&#x000a0;inert, it&#x000a0;contributes substantially to&#x000a0;atmospheric and alveolar stability (see<bold>&#x000a0;</bold><bold>Images&#x000a0;</bold>Components&#x000a0;of Air and Partial Pressure at STPD (1 atm, 0 &#x000b0;C, 0% Humidity) and Partial Pressures of Respiratory Gases in Air Samples and Blood).</p>
        <p>Animals, as well as most bacteria, fungi, and protozoa, require oxygen for aerobic metabolism and energy production. In single-celled organisms, oxygen delivery occurs by simple diffusion. This process was first quantitatively described by Fick's first law of diffusion, which states that the rate of gas transfer across a biological membrane is proportional to the pressure gradient, molecular weight,&#x000a0;and solubility of the gases involved. Additionally, the&#x000a0;membrane's structural properties, specifically related to its thickness and surface area, play a significant role in oxygen transport. However, in the universe of multicellular animals, simple diffusion is routinely insufficient to meet the organism's complex and increasing metabolic demands. To support the increased oxygen requirements of advanced species, large amounts of oxygen&#x000a0;must be efficiently transported through the bloodstream, where they bind to hemoglobin, a complex iron-containing protein. Once in the bloodstream, oxygen must be delivered effectively to vital tissues through convective flow, which is produced by the heart's contractions and measured as cardiac output (CO). Oxygen consumption or delivery, as well as oxygen content within blood, may be impaired by various disease processes that directly or indirectly disrupt pulmonary gas exchange, oxygen transport, and tissue utilization.&#x000a0;Ultimately, one or more of these pathologic processes may lead to cellular or tissue hypoxia due to an imbalance between oxygen delivery (DO<sub>2</sub>) and oxygen&#x000a0;consumption (VO<sub>2</sub>). Thus, cellular or tissue hypoxia may be defined,&#x000a0;with or without systemic hypoxemia, as a relative imbalance between oxygen delivery and the underlying cellular&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;tissue demand for this vital energy substrate.</p>
        <p>Effective interpretation of the partial pressure of oxygen requires a thorough understanding of the fundamental gas laws, as well as the concepts governing&#x000a0;gas exchange, oxygen diffusion, and cellular or membrane transport. This review explores in great detail the physiologic mechanisms and clinical significance of the partial pressure of oxygen. In particular,&#x000a0;it highlights&#x000a0;the pivotal role of the partial pressure of oxygen in determining the ultimate arterial&#x000a0;or venous saturation of hemoglobin in red blood cells (RBCs). This fundamental relationship underpins much of clinical practice in arterial saturation monitoring&#x02014;whether through arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis or continuous pulse oximetry (SpO<sub>2</sub>)&#x02014;across disciplines such as anesthesiology, critical care medicine, and pulmonary medicine.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26710.s2" sec-type="Function">
        <title>Function</title>
        <p>
<bold>Blood-Gas Barrier</bold>
</p>
        <p>Gas exchange within the mammalian lung occurs&#x000a0;along the length of the&#x000a0;alveolar-capillary membrane (ie, the blood-gas barrier). Collectively,&#x000a0;the alveolar-capillary&#x000a0;tissue mass accounts for&#x000a0;approximately 90% of the lung parenchyma and encompasses a surface area of&#x000a0;approximately&#x000a0;70&#x000a0;to 100 m<sup>2</sup>. Notably,<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>this vast surface area approximates the&#x000a0;size of a standard tennis court. This&#x000a0;extensive&#x000a0;surface area, combined with&#x000a0;a very thin&#x000a0;alveolar-capillary membrane (approximately 0.3-0.5&#x000a0;&#x000b5;m&#x000a0;thick),&#x000a0;enables&#x000a0;rapid gas exchange&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;substantial&#x000a0;diffusion reserve (see <bold>Image.</bold> Alveolar Membrane Showing Barriers to Gas Diffusion From Air to Blood).</p>
        <p>On average, it takes an RBC only 0.75 seconds to travel from the venous circulation to the arterial side of the capillaries surrounding the alveoli. The&#x000a0;partial pressure of capillary oxygen (PacO<sub>2</sub>)&#x000a0;within the RBC (ie, capillary blood) equilibrates with the partial pressure of&#x000a0;alveolar oxygen (PAO<sub>2</sub>) inside the&#x000a0;first&#x000a0;one-third&#x000a0;of the capillary's length (see <bold>Image.</bold> PacO<sub>2</sub>&#x000a0;Equilibrates With PAO<sub>2</sub>&#x000a0;Within the First One-Third of the Alveolar Capillary).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Diffusion</bold>
</p>
        <p>Historically, the delivery of oxygen from the atmosphere to the organism's pulmonary circulation was believed to be an active,&#x000a0;energy-dependent process located in or adjacent to the alveolar spaces.&#x000a0;In 1855,&#x000a0;Adolf Fick made a fundamental breakthrough by challenging this outdated concept and modeling gas movement using physical laws analogous to Fourier's law of heat conduction. Professor Fick demonstrated that oxygen transfer occurred as a passive flux down a concentration gradient. This work led to the formulation of Fick's first law of diffusion, which quantitatively described the diffusion of gases across biological&#x000a0;membranes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r1">[1]</xref> In 1920, August and Marie Krogh were awarded the Nobel Prize for their groundbreaking research, which demonstrated that oxygen&#x000a0;diffuses&#x000a0;across the pulmonary alveolar-capillary membrane into blood and later into cells and tissues by passive diffusion driven by these concentration gradients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Fick's&#x000a0;first&#x000a0;law&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;diffusion:</bold> Fick's first law of diffusion describes the rate of diffusion of a substance across a membrane as:</p>
        <p>J = &#x02212;D (dC/dx)</p>
        <p>Where:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>J = diffusive flux (amount transferred per unit area per unit time)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>D = diffusion coefficient of the gas</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>dC/dx = concentration gradient across the membrane</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The negative sign indicates movement from&#x000a0;higher to&#x000a0;lower concentration.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Henry Law</bold>
</p>
        <p>Henry law states that the concentration of a gas dissolved in a liquid is directly proportional to the gas's solubility in the liquid and its partial pressure above the liquid. Inspired oxygen diffuses across the thin, liquid-lined alveolar-capillary membrane&#x000a0;into the capillary blood, where the resulting partial pressure of dissolved oxygen determines the degree of oxygen-hemoglobin binding and saturation. Importantly, it is the dissolved oxygen tension (PaO<sub>2</sub>)&#x02014;rather than total oxygen content&#x02014;that drives hemoglobin saturation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>C = k(P)&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Where:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>C&#x000a0;= concentration of the dissolved gas</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>k&#x000a0;= solubility coefficient</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>P&#x000a0;= partial pressure of the gas</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Therefore, either concentration or pressure gradients can be used in physiology (see <bold>Image.</bold> Henry Law).</p>
        <p>Because flux represents the&#x000a0;rate of gas transfer per unit surface area, Fick's&#x000a0;first law of diffusion can be reformulated to express the total gas transfer rate (Vgas) as a function of the membrane surface area, membrane thickness, the diffusion properties of the gas and the membrane, and the gas concentration gradient. Using Henry law, the gas concentration gradient can be expressed in terms of the pressure gradient. &#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Vgas = (A / T) &#x000d7; D &#x000d7; (PAO<sub>2</sub> &#x02212; PacO<sub>2</sub>)</p>
        <p>&#x000a0;Where:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Vgas = rate of gas transfer across the membrane per unit time</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A = surface area available for diffusion</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>T = membrane thickness</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>D = diffusion coefficient of the gas</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>PAO<sub>2</sub> = partial pressure of alveolar oxygen</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>PacO<sub>2</sub> = Partial pressure of capillary oxygen</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Perfusion</bold>
</p>
        <p>Notably, oxygenation of arterial blood through the pulmonary circulation depends not only on diffusion but also on the corresponding perfusion. As mentioned previously, an RBC takes approximately 0.75 seconds to pass through the alveolar capillary. However, during exercise, the increase in the individual's cardiac output actually shortens the transit time substantially to around 0.25 seconds. In the absence of diffusion limitations,&#x000a0;the rate of oxygen uptake within alveolar capillaries is driven by pulmonary blood flow. However, in conditions such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) (eg, emphysema) and interstitial lung disease (eg, pulmonary fibrosis), the flow of blood within the capillaries far exceeds the&#x000a0;diffusion capacity of oxygen itself. This relative mismatch prevents the partial pressure of alveolar oxygen from equilibrating effectively with the partial pressure of capillary oxygen (PacO<sub>2</sub>), leading to systemic or regional hypoxemia in the most severe cases.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ventilation and Perfusion Relationship</bold>
</p>
        <p>The ratio of pulmonary ventilation and perfusion in an ideal lung is 1 (V/Q = 1). However, ventilation and perfusion may vary across various regional zones from the apex to the base of the lung (see <bold>Image.</bold> West Lung Zones).</p>
        <p>Zone 1 (apex): Ventilation/perfusion &#x0003e; 1&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Zone 2 (middle): Ventilation/perfusion = 1&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Zone 3 (lung bases): Ventilation/perfusion &#x0003c; 1</p>
        <p>Due largely to the effects of gravity, greater pulmonary blood flow and resultant volumes are present in the bases of the lungs compared with those at the apices, where ventilation far exceeds perfusion. Consequently, the net V/Q is around 0.8, reflecting alveolar ventilation of approximately 4 L/min and pulmonary blood of 5 L/min (4/5 = 0.8). This value results directly from the admixture of pulmonary capillary blood from all zones draining into the 4 pulmonary veins.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Physiological Shunt&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Approximately 1% to 2% of total cardiac output bypasses the pulmonary circulation, further contributing to the mixing of venous blood and affecting both anatomical and physiological shunting.</p>
        <p>Collectively,&#x000a0;as described above, V/Q relationships, along with the amount of anatomical and physiological shunting, ultimately determine the gradient between the PAO<sub>2</sub> and PaO<sub>2</sub>. The normal A-a gradient is approximately 10 to 15 mmHg and increases with age.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Predicted A-a gradient = (age / 10) + 4</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>PO<sub>2</sub> and Oxygen Transport</bold>
</p>
        <p>In the early 1900s,&#x000a0;Archibald Vivian Hill was the first to describe and mathematically prove&#x000a0;that oxygen binding to hemoglobin is a cooperative, reversible process determined&#x000a0;by&#x000a0;equilibrium&#x000a0;with PaO<sub>2</sub>. The Hill equation also formed the sigmoid-shaped oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>SHbO<sub>2</sub> = [HbO<sub>2</sub>] / [Hb]&#x000a0;&#x021d2; KHb [O<sub>2</sub>]<sup>n</sup> / 1 + KHb [O<sub>2</sub>]<sup>n</sup></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Where:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>SHbO<sub>2</sub> = fraction of oxygen-bound hemoglobin</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>HbO<sub>2</sub> = concentration of oxygen-bound hemoglobin</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hb = hemoglobin concentration (Hct)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>n = Hill coefficient&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Normally, the Hill coefficient is around 2.56 &#x000b1; 0.07.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>n &#x0003e; 1:&#x000a0;Indicates&#x000a0;positive cooperativity, where higher PaO<sub>2</sub> levels enhance O<sub>2</sub> binding, as binding of 1 O<sub>2</sub> molecule increases the affinity for&#x000a0;subsequent&#x000a0;molecules.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>n &#x0003c; 1: Reflects negative cooperativity.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>n = 1:&#x000a0;Represents&#x000a0;non-cooperative binding, with independent binding events producing a hyperbolic binding curve.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Putting these principles together, arterial blood oxygen content is defined as:&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>CaO<sub>2</sub> = (1.39 &#x000d7; Hb &#x000d7;&#x000a0;SaO<sub>2</sub>) + (0.003 &#x000d7;&#x000a0;PaO<sub>2</sub>)&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Where:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hb = hemoglobin concentration (g/dL)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>SaO<sub>2</sub> = arterial oxygen saturation (fraction)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>PaO<sub>2</sub> = partial pressure of arterial oxygen (mmHg)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>1.34 = mL of O<sub>2</sub> bound per gram of hemoglobin&#x000a0;(1.39 in ideal stoichiometry)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>0.003 = mL of dissolved O<sub>2</sub> per dL blood per mmHg&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Normal arterial oxygen content bound to hemoglobin is roughly 20 mL O<sub>2</sub>/dL (range: 17-20 mL O<sub>2</sub>/dL), compared with 0.003 mL of dissolved O<sub>2</sub>/dL blood per mmHg. At STPD (1 atm, 0 &#x000b0;c, 0% humidity), with a normal blood temperature of 37 &#x000b0;C and a PaO<sub>2</sub> of approximately 100 mmHg, the amount of oxygen dissolved in blood is roughly 0.3 mL O<sub>2</sub>/dL. Hence, PaO<sub>2</sub> contributes minimally to the blood's oxygen content (CaO<sub>2</sub>) and oxygen delivery (DO<sub>2</sub>) under normal conditions. However, PaO<sub>2</sub> becomes the critical determinant of tissue oxygenation when it falls below the mandatory physiologic threshold. This threshold is typically around 55&#x000a0;to 60 mmHg. Violation of this boundary results in a rapid decline in hemoglobin-oxygen saturation, as predicted by the steep portion of the sigmoid dissociation curve (see <bold>Image.</bold> Hemoglobin Dissociation Curve)<bold>.</bold></p>
        <p>Humans are engineered to deliver oxygen (DO<sub>2</sub>) at approximately 4-fold the rate&#x000a0;of oxygen consumption, providing a&#x000a0;substantial oxygen reserve to maintain aerobic metabolism during physiologic stress. Therefore, under routine conditions, tissues within healthy individuals normally extract only 20% to 25% of delivered oxygen. This proportion is expressed as the O<sub>2</sub> extraction ratio (O<sub>2</sub>ER):</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VO<sub>2</sub> = 250 mL O<sub>2</sub> / min</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>DO<sub>2</sub> = 1000 mL O<sub>2</sub> / min</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>When DO<sub>2</sub> cannot meet metabolic demands, oxidative phosphorylation relies on anaerobic metabolism, leading to lactic acidosis and tissue injury. Oxygen delivery (DO<sub>2</sub>) is quantitatively expressed by&#x000a0;multiplying CaO<sub>2</sub>&#x000a0;by cardiac output (HR &#x000d7; SV), which is commonly found to be in the range of 1000 mL O<sub>2</sub>/min:</p>
        <p>DO<sub>2</sub> = CaO<sub>2</sub> &#x000d7; CO</p>
        <p>Since cardiac output is measured in liters&#x000a0;per minute (L/min),&#x000a0;multiplying&#x000a0;by a factor of 10 converts DO<sub>2</sub> units to mL/min.</p>
        <p>Oxygen consumption (VO<sub>2</sub>) is the rate of systemic oxygen consumption as defined by the Fick principle&#x000a0;under steady-state conditions. Notably, oxygen consumption can be expressed similarly whether looking at the level of the lungs or tissue measurements themselves:</p>
        <p>
<bold>Systemic (tissue) oxygen consumption:</bold>
</p>
        <p>VO<sub>2</sub>, systemic = CO &#x000d7; (CaO<sub>2</sub> &#x02212; CvO<sub>2</sub>)</p>
        <p>Where:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>CO = cardiac output</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>CaO<sub>2</sub> = arterial oxygen content</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>CvO<sub>2</sub> = venous oxygen content</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Pulmonary (lung) oxygen uptake:</bold> Defined by&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;mass balance of inspired and expired oxygen:</p>
        <p>VO<sub>2</sub>, lungs= (VAI &#x000d7; FIO<sub>2</sub>) &#x02212; (VAE &#x000d7; FEO<sub>2</sub>)&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Where:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VAI = inspired alveolar ventilation (oxygen entering the lungs per unit time)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>VAE = expired alveolar ventilation (oxygen leaving the lungs per unit time)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>FIO<sub>2</sub> = fraction of oxygen in inspired gas</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>FEO<sub>2</sub> = fraction of oxygen in expired gas</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>At this point, it is&#x000a0;evident&#x000a0;that many clinical and pathophysiologic factors influence PO<sub>2</sub> at various stages of oxygen diffusion. The following section discusses these factors and the clinical implications of the partial pressure of oxygen (PO<sub>2</sub>) within the human body, tissues, and cells.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26710.s3" sec-type="Issues of Concern">
        <title>Issues of Concern</title>
        <p>
<bold>Issues of Concern</bold>
</p>
        <p>In most clinical settings, blood gas analysis is used to diagnose and manage acute respiratory failure;&#x000a0;however,&#x000a0;it should never delay treatment. The clinical utility of measuring or calculating the partial pressure of oxygen is related to the diagnosis and grading&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;the severity of&#x000a0;hypoxemic respiratory&#x000a0;failure. For example, ABG sampling is indicated routinely for patients who present with severe hypercapnia,&#x000a0;severe respiratory acidosis, or who require extracorporeal life support with oxygenators.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>However, arterial puncture can be painful and may cause complications, unnecessary resource use, and higher healthcare costs. For example, Chandran et al reported that routine ABG protocols did not alter clinical management and were associated with&#x000a0;increased costs&#x000a0;and resource&#x000a0;utilization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Walsh et al reported that implementing an ABG sampling clinical pathway in the&#x000a0;intensive care unit (ICU) resulted in a 32.1% reduction in ABG tests, with no difference in severity of illness, duration of mechanical ventilation, or mortality&#x000a0;rates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>The diagnostic use and interpretation of hypoxemia and hypoxia can be ambiguous; therefore, distinguishing between the two is essential.&#x000a0;Hypoxemia refers to critically low PaO<sub>2 </sub>or hemoglobin oxygen saturation (SpO<sub>2</sub>) that requires supplemental oxygen to&#x000a0;maintain blood oxygenation, typically defined as SpO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003c; 90% or&#x000a0;PaO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003c; 60 mmHg. Hypoxia is the clinical state in which the delivery of oxygen (DO<sub>2</sub>) is insufficient to meet the metabolic demands of cells&#x000a0;or tissues for aerobic metabolism (VO<sub>2</sub>). Thus, hypoxia is analogous to shock in that it may result from anemia, severe hypoxemia, low tissue perfusion pressure, low cardiac output, or mitochondrial dysfunction (eg, cyanide poisoning).&#x000a0;Importantly, hypoxia is a clinical diagnosis rather than low SpO<sub>2</sub>&#x000a0;or PaO<sub>2</sub>, as in hypoxemia. Therefore, caution should be exercised when diagnosing acute hypoxemic or hypoxic respiratory failure, and a comprehensive review of all applicable respiratory parameters should be conducted.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Another area&#x000a0;of ongoing controversy is how to administer supplemental oxygen to&#x000a0;maintain&#x000a0;an acceptable SpO<sub>2</sub> while minimizing the risk of oxygen toxicity, as discussed further in the next section.&#x000a0;Judicious use of supplemental oxygen requires healthcare&#x000a0;providers to understand that administering oxygen is no different from&#x000a0;providing any other medication. At times, this may require limiting or restricting its routine use in patients who do not need it. Additionally, comprehensive education remains necessary to ensure proper use and application of oxygen by healthcare providers. Such education&#x000a0;underpins the key concept of oxygen stewardship and the appropriate administration and use of oxygen within the healthcare environment.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26710.s4" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>
<bold>Causes of Hypoxemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Five&#x000a0;causes of hypoxemia&#x000a0;include:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Low inspired partial pressure of oxygen (PIO<sub>2</sub>)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypoventilation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Diffusion limitation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>V/Q mismatch</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Right-to-left shunt&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The most common causes&#x000a0;of hypoxemia include V/Q mismatch and right-to-left shunting of blood through areas of low V/Q (V/Q &#x0003c; 1). Hypoxemia due to a diffuse limitation is less common and occurs in patients with severe pulmonary disease (eg, pulmonary fibrosis or emphysema). However, as mentioned previously, increased cardiac output during vigorous exercise reduces the transit time of blood significantly through the pulmonary capillaries. This&#x000a0;shortened transit time naturally limits oxygen diffusion across alveolar membranes in patients with underlying pulmonary disease,&#x000a0;who are most in need of effective oxygen transport.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Significance of&#x000a0;the Partial Pressure of Oxygen (PO<sub>2</sub>)&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Partial pressure of arterial oxygen (PAO<sub>2</sub>):</bold> To maintain a sufficient PaO<sub>2</sub> for hemoglobin oxygen saturation,&#x000a0;a constant driving pressure across the alveolar-capillary membrane is required to maintain passive diffusion of O<sub>2</sub> into capillary blood, as modeled by Fick's first law of diffusion. In respiratory physiology, this gradient is the difference between the partial pressure of arterial oxygen (PAO<sub>2</sub>) and the partial pressure of pulmonary capillary oxygen (PacO<sub>2</sub>). Normally,&#x000a0;PAO<sub>2</sub> is equal to PacO<sub>2</sub> at the end of the pulmonary capillary.</p>
        <p>Unlike CO<sub>2</sub>, where PaCO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248; PACO<sub>2</sub>,&#x000a0;both oxygen consumption (VO<sub>2</sub>) and CO<sub>2</sub> production (VCO<sub>2</sub>) influence alveolar oxygen tension directly, as&#x000a0;described&#x000a0;by the respiratory quotient: RQ = VCO<sub>2</sub>/VO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248;&#x000a0;0.8&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Respiratory quotient&#x000a0;(RQ):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Glucose = C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> + 6O<sub>2</sub> &#x02192; 6CO<sub>2</sub> + 6H<sub>2</sub>O + 38ATP + heat</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>RQ = rate of CO<sub>2</sub> production/rate of O<sub>2</sub> consumption&#x000a0;&#x021d2;&#x000a0;6CO<sub>2</sub> / 6O<sub>2</sub> = 1</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Alveolar ventilation removes CO<sub>2</sub> and delivers oxygen-rich gas to the alveoli, maintaining a relatively stable PAO<sub>2</sub>. The functional residual capacity (FRC) acts as an oxygen reservoir, buffering changes in alveolar oxygen tension.</p>
        <p>If glucose is the sole metabolic substrate undergoing aerobic metabolism, the RQ equals 1.0. However, because typical Western diets include carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, the average RQ is approximately 0.8. This value is&#x000a0;central&#x000a0;to&#x000a0;the alveolar gas equation.</p>
        <p>Notably, the volume of exhaled air is slightly greater than the volume of inspired air. This difference is primarily because inspired air&#x000a0;is warmed by body temperature and saturated with water vapor,&#x000a0;thereby expanding the gas. Additionally, expired air&#x000a0;contains&#x000a0;CO<sub>2</sub> produced&#x000a0;by&#x000a0;cellular metabolism,&#x000a0;thereby&#x000a0;altering&#x000a0;its&#x000a0;gas composition&#x000a0;relative&#x000a0;to inspired air. Collectively, these physical and compositional changes account for the modest increase in expired gas volumes.</p>
        <p>Accordingly:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VAI = inspired alveolar ventilation (oxygen entering the lungs per unit time)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>VAE = expired alveolar ventilation (oxygen leaving the lungs per unit time)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>FIO<sub>2</sub> = fraction of oxygen in inspired gas&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>FEO<sub>2</sub> = fraction of oxygen in expired gas</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Because N<sub>2</sub> is a physiologically inert gas that does not participate in pulmonary gas exchange, the quantity of nitrogen inhaled equals the quantity exhaled under steady-state conditions. Therefore, the rate of nitrogen inspired (VIN<sub>2</sub>) equals the quantity of exhaled (VEN<sub>2</sub>):<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VIN<sub>2</sub> = VEN<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>By this principle, inspired and expired alveolar ventilations (VAI and VAE) can be calculated from the measured fractional concentrations of nitrogen in the inspired and expired gases (FIN<sub>2</sub> and FEN<sub>2</sub>).&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Since the partial pressure of CO<sub>2</sub> in atmospheric air (Patm-CO<sub>2</sub>) is effectively zero in respiratory physiology, and because CO<sub>2</sub> diffuses rapidly across the alveolar-capillary membrane, PACO<sub>2</sub> is primarily a function of alveolar ventilation, as described by the alveolar gas equation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Derivation of the Alveolar Ventilation Equation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Tidal volume is composed of&#x000a0;2 components:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VT = VD + VA&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Where:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VD = dead&#x000a0;space</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>VA = alveolar volume</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Because dead-space&#x000a0;gas is essentially atmospheric air that does not participate in gas exchange, the fraction of CO<sub>2&#x000a0;</sub>in dead space is negligible:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VD &#x000d7; FatmCO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248; 0</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Therefore, the total expired CO<sub>2</sub> volume per breath is equal to the alveolar CO<sub>2</sub> volume:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VTCO<sub>2</sub> = VACO<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Multiplying by respiratory rate converts per-breath values to per-minute values or&#x000a0;minute ventilation (VE):&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VECO<sub>2</sub> = VACO<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Since:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VACO<sub>2</sub> = VA &#x000d7; FACO<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Then:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>FACO<sub>2</sub> = VCO<sub>2</sub>/VA</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Converting fractional concentration to partial pressure yields:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PACO<sub>2</sub> = K &#x000d7; (VCO<sub>2</sub>/VAE)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Under steady-state conditions:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PACO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248; PaCO<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Where:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VAE = expired alveolar ventilation (oxygen leaving the lungs per unit time)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In summary, PACO<sub>2</sub> is&#x000a0;directly related to the metabolic production rate of CO<sub>2</sub> and inversely related to&#x000a0;the alveolar&#x000a0;ventilation. However, unlike CO<sub>2</sub>, where PaCO<sub>2</sub> approximates PACO<sub>2</sub>, PAO<sub>2</sub> is determined by atmospheric pressure, the partial pressures of other alveolar gases, temperature-dependent water vapor pressures, and the respiratory quotient (RQ). Additionally, it is not possible to directly collect alveolar gases to measure PAO<sub>2</sub>. The alveolar gas&#x000a0;equation allows us to estimate the alveolar partial pressure of oxygen (PAO<sub>2</sub>)&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;to assess the factors contributing to arterial oxygen tension (PaO<sub>2</sub>).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Derivation of the Alveolar Gas Equation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Nitrogen balance (Haldane principle):</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>VIN<sub>2</sub> = VEN<sub>2</sub> &#x021d2; VN<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>VN<sub>2</sub> = VAI &#x000d7; FIN<sub>2</sub> &#x021d2;&#x000a0;VAI = VN<sub>2</sub>/FIN<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>VN<sub>2</sub> = VAE &#x000d7; FEN<sub>2</sub> &#x021d2;&#x000a0;VAE = VN<sub>2</sub>/FEN<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Alveolar gas fractions (Dalton framework):&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Inspired alveolar gas: 1 = FIAN<sub>2</sub> + FIAO<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Expired alveolar gas: 1= FEAN<sub>2</sub> + FEAO<sub>2</sub> + FEACO<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Where:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>FEA = fraction of expired alveolar gas</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>FIA = fraction of inspired alveolar gas</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>RQ in terms of alveolar ventilation and fractions:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>RQ = VCO<sub>2</sub>/VO<sub>2</sub> = VAE (1 &#x02212; FEAN<sub>2</sub> &#x02212; FEAO<sub>2</sub>) / VAI (1 &#x02212; FIAN<sub>2</sub>)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Solving for FAO<sub>2</sub> results in:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>FAO<sub>2</sub> = FAIO<sub>2</sub> &#x02212; FAECO<sub>2</sub> [FAIO<sub>2</sub> + (1 &#x02212; FAIO<sub>2</sub>) / RQ]</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Expansion of the term in parentheses:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>1/RQ &#x02212; [FAIO<sub>2</sub> (1 &#x02212; RQ)] / RQ&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Consider a patient on a Western diet (RQ = 0.8) and breathing on room air (FIO<sub>2</sub>&#x000a0;= 0.21)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>[0.21 (1 &#x02212; 0.8)] / 0.8 = 0.05 &#x02192; effectively cancels out, leaving &#x02192; 1/RQ</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Simplified equation:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>FAO<sub>2</sub> = FIO<sub>2</sub> &#x02212; (FACO<sub>2</sub> / RQ)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Conversion to partial pressure by multiplying times barometric pressure (Patm) &#x02212; 47 mmHg water at sea level (760 mmHg &#x02212; 47 mmHg H<sub>2</sub>O)</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PAO<sub>2</sub> = FIO<sub>2</sub> (760 &#x02212; 47) &#x02212; (PACO<sub>2</sub> / RQ)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Since PACO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248; PaCO<sub>2</sub>:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PAO<sub>2</sub> = FIO<sub>2</sub> (760 &#x02212; 47) &#x02212; (PaCO<sub>2</sub> / RQ)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The final alveolar gas equation:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PAO<sub>2</sub> = FIO<sub>2</sub> (Patm &#x02212; 47) &#x02212; (PaCO<sub>2</sub>/0.8)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Examples of clinical factors affecting PAO<sub>2</sub>, as explained by the alveolar gas equation:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>FIO<sub>2</sub>: Suffocation, airway obstruction, and supplemental oxygen</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Atmospheric pressure&#x000a0;(Patm, 760 mmHg&#x000a0;at sea level): Changes with altitude and application of positive pressure ventilation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Heated water vapor (PH<sub>2</sub>O&#x000a0;&#x02248; 45 mmHg): Humidification in&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;ventilator circuit&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypercapnia (increased PaCO<sub>2</sub>): Hypercapnic respiratory failure, reduced MVe, and elevated VCO<sub>2</sub></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>RQ (respiratory quotient): Ketogenic diet &#x02192; RQ &#x0003c; 0.7</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Partial Pressure of Arterial Oxygen (PaO<sub>2</sub>)</bold>
</p>
        <p>As previously mentioned,&#x000a0;a normal gradient exists between PAO<sub>2</sub> and PaO<sub>2</sub> (approximately 10 to 15 mmHg) due to V/Q mismatching (V/Q &#x0003c; 1) and physiological shunts.</p>
        <p>A&#x000a0;high&#x000a0;A-a gradient is indicative of V/Q mismatch, right-to-left shunt, or diffusion&#x000a0;limitation, and helps distinguish hypoxemia due to hypoventilation or low PIO<sub>2</sub>. Additionally, the A-a gradient provides clinicians with useful diagnostic insight. The ability to trend these values over time, especially in critically ill patients, frequently provides useful insight into their clinical outcomes. Specifically, objective data collection and interpretation in cases of acute hypoxemic respiratory failure, as well as assessment of the response to endotracheal intubation and the institution of positive-pressure mechanical ventilation, determine, in large part, the patient's outcome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Over the past 3 decades, the PaO<sub>2</sub>/FIO<sub>2</sub> (P/F) ratio has become a straightforward and widely used clinical index for grading the severity of systemic hypoxemia. The clinical relevance of the P/F ratio is particularly evident when the clinician is called on to manage advanced lung pathology, such as acute lung injury (ALI) and acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).&#x000a0;In 2012, the Berlin Diagnostic Criteria for ARDS included P/F as a consensus criterion. Importantly, this P/F inclusion criterion helped the clinician stratify patients presenting with ARDS into mild (200-300 mmHg), moderate (100-200 mmHg), and severe (&#x0003c;100 mmHg) categories, with&#x000a0;a minimum positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) of&#x000a0;5 cm&#x000a0;H<sub>2</sub>O. The P/F severity categories also correlate closely with the ultimate manifestation of mortality. Hence, the P/F ratio has gradually evolved to benefit patient management by serving as both a diagnostic and prognostic tool when stratifying patients with ALI and ARDS. For example, in subsequent prospective, double-blind research studies, the aforementioned Berlin validation cohorts observed&#x000a0;mortality rates of 27% for mild, 32% for moderate, and 45% for severe P/F disturbances.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Measurement of PaO<sub>2</sub></bold>
</p>
        <p>Infrared analyzers or paramagnetic analyzers can measure expired PO<sub>2</sub>; however, unlike end-tidal PCO<sub>2</sub>, end-tidal PO<sub>2</sub> overrepresents well-ventilated alveolar units and does not&#x000a0;represent&#x000a0;poorly ventilated and shunted alveoli.&#x000a0;Therefore, the alveolar gas equation is used to estimate the PAO<sub>2</sub>.</p>
        <p>Leland Clark first developed the oxygen electrode to measure blood oxygen tension, using a platinum wire fused into glass and coated with cellophane. Oxygen in blood is reduced by platinum, generating an electrical current that is converted into pressure (mmHg) via calibration curves.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>O<sub>2</sub> + 4e<sup>&#x02212;</sup>&#x000a0;+ 4H &#x02192; 2H<sub>2</sub>O</p>
        <p>Because the platinum cathode consumed oxygen rapidly, the blood sample required vigorous stirring. In 1956,&#x000a0;Severinghaus et al developed a cuvette within a thermostat, further revolutionizing the technology of blood gas analyzers used in daily clinical practice.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Partial Pressure of Venous O<sub>2</sub> (PvO<sub>2</sub>)</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Interpretation of PvO<sub>2</sub>:</bold> Oxygen is delivered to tissue by convection (ie, cardiac output) and diffusion from capillaries, as described by Fick's&#x000a0;first law of diffusion:</p>
        <p>QO<sub>2</sub> = (A / T) &#x000d7;&#x000a0;Dk&#x000a0;(PacO<sub>2</sub> &#x02013; PO<sub>2</sub> cell)</p>
        <p>Where:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>CO = cardiac&#x000a0;output (blood L/min)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>QO<sub>2</sub> = O<sub>2</sub> L/min&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A = surface area available for diffusion&#x000a0;around&#x000a0;the capillary&#x000a0;tissue</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>T (or dx) = thickness or distance between capillaries and tissues&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>PacO<sub>2</sub> = partial pressure of oxygen within capillaries</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>PO<sub>2</sub> cell = partial pressure of oxygen within the cells of tissue</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>This equation shares similar physiologic principles with diffusion across the alveolar-capillary membrane. Since O<sub>2</sub> must diffuse from the capillaries through interstitial fluid and cells to reach the mitochondria, the diffusion distance is longer than that of the alveolar-capillary membrane (approximately 50&#x000a0;&#x000b5;m versus 0.5 &#x000b5;m). For example, during exercise, arterioles and capillaries dilate, increasing DO<sub>2</sub> and the surface area available for diffusion. The thickness or&#x000a0;distance between capillaries and cells has pathophysiologic implications; for example, increased interstitial edema lengthens the diffusion distance, reducing QO<sub>2&#x000a0;</sub>(see <bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Physics of Oxygen Diffusion from Atmosphere to Mitochondria).</p>
        <p>Importantly, muscle tissue&#x000a0;contains&#x000a0;myoglobin, which serves as a cellular oxygen reservoir and enhances the muscle's ability to sustain aerobic metabolism during&#x000a0;physiological stress. Myoglobin's oxygen binding results in a cellular PO<sub>2</sub> in muscle of around 1&#x000a0;to 3 mmHg,&#x000a0;maintaining a strong concentration gradient for diffusion. Unlike hemoglobin, myoglobin binds O<sub>2</sub> by simple saturation (see <bold>Image. </bold>Oxygen Transport by the Proteins Myoglobin and Hemoglobin). At lower PO<sub>2</sub>, myoglobin has a higher oxygen-binding affinity, thereby further enhancing diffusion from blood to tissue.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>As previously discussed, oxygen consumption (VO<sub>2</sub>) is the rate of systemic oxygen consumption as defined by the Fick equation (principle) under steady-state conditions. Hence, oxygen consumption is the difference between arterial oxygen content (CaO<sub>2</sub>)&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;venous oxygen content:</p>
        <p>VO<sub>2</sub>, systemic = CO &#x000d7; (CaO<sub>2</sub> &#x02212; CvO<sub>2</sub>)&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Where:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>CO = cardiac output</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>CaO<sub>2</sub> = arterial oxygen content&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>CvO<sub>2</sub> = venous oxygen content</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>As cardiac output and hemoglobin&#x000a0;are the same in arterial and venous circulation,&#x000a0;CaO<sub>2</sub> &#x02013; CvO<sub>2</sub> is simplified to:</p>
        <p>VO<sub>2</sub> = [CO &#x000d7; 1.39 &#x000d7; Hgb (SaO<sub>2</sub> &#x02013; SvO<sub>2</sub>)] + [0.003 (PaO<sub>2</sub> &#x02013; PvO<sub>2</sub>)] &#x000d7; 10</p>
        <p>Of significant importance is the observation that the oxygen content of venous blood returning to the right atrium is slightly lower than that returning from the inferior vena cava versus that measured from within the superior vena cava (SVC). This difference is true because the heart itself extracts a significant amount of oxygen to support its high energy substrate consumption.&#x000a0;Hence, the low-oxygen blood from the coronary sinus mixes with venous blood returning from the inferior vena cava. This mixing&#x000a0;ultimately results in a lower mixed venous oxygen content than that found within the central venous oxygen content of the inferior vena cava.</p>
        <p>Normally:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>CvO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248;15 mL/dL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>SvO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248; 75%&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>ScvO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248; 80%&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>PvO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248; 40 mmHg</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Venous blood gases may serve as an alternative to approximate arterial pH and PCO<sub>2</sub>, but the difference and variability between venous oxygen tension (PvO<sub>2</sub>) and PaO<sub>2</sub> are too large to approximate PaO<sub>2</sub> levels on the scale of&#x000a0;55 mmHg.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;However,&#x000a0;Vincet&#x000a0;JL et al reported&#x000a0;that low SVO<sub>2</sub> is an important clinical warning sign for critically low DO<sub>2</sub>,&#x000a0;whereas&#x000a0;normal or elevated values do not rule out tissue hypoxia (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Partial Pressures of Respiratory Gases in Air Samples and Blood).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Supplemental Oxygen</bold>
</p>
        <p>Nasal cannula:&#x000a0;Routinely delivers&#x000a0;up to 8 L/min of oxygen. The administered FIO<sub>2</sub> increases by approximately 4% for each 1 L/min increase in flow rate. For example, 8 L/min provides an FIO<sub>2</sub> of approximately 0.45. However, patients with tachypnea have inspiratory flow rates far exceeding 8 L/min,&#x000a0;thereby&#x000a0;diluting&#x000a0;the supplemental FIO<sub>2</sub>.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Face mask:&#x000a0;Covers the mouth and nose with a poor seal but reduces mixing with ambient air. The administered FIO<sub>2</sub> is&#x000a0;approximately 0.35&#x000a0;to 0.60, with a maximum flow rate of 5 L/min.</p>
        <p>Face tent:&#x000a0;Similar to&#x000a0;a face&#x000a0;mask,&#x000a0;but&#x000a0;provides greater control over humidified oxygen concentration. Flow rates of 5 to 15 L/min can provide an FIO<sub>2</sub> of up to 0.50.</p>
        <p>Nonrebreather face mask:&#x000a0;A face mask with an oxygen reservoir bag. The reservoir bag&#x000a0;contains&#x000a0;100% FIO<sub>2</sub> and is equipped with a one-way inspiratory valve that prevents dilution&#x000a0;by expired&#x000a0;air. The nonrebreather face mask delivers a flow rate of 10&#x000a0;to 15 L/min, resulting in a maximal FIO<sub>2</sub> of 0.9.</p>
        <p>Venturi mask: Delivers flow rates up to 15 L/min via jet-mixing to entrain and mix air with oxygen, with a&#x000a0;maximal&#x000a0;FIO<sub>2</sub> of approximately 0.50.</p>
        <p>High-flow nasal cannula:&#x000a0;Delivers O<sub>2</sub> with heat-humidified air and flow rates up to 70 L/min. High-flow nasal cannula is ideal for patients with high inspiratory flow rates and minute ventilation because it matches these flow rates, thereby reducing mixing with ambient air. FIO<sub>2</sub> from a high-flow nasal cannula approaches 100% and provides modest PEEP, making it ideal for patients with pulmonary edema, pneumonia, and atelectasis (see <bold>Image.</bold> O<sub>2&#x000a0;</sub>Flow and FIO<sub>2</sub>).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Mechanical Ventilation</bold>
</p>
        <p>The FIO<sub>2</sub> delivered by positive-pressure ventilation, especially invasive positive-pressure ventilation, is less diluted by ambient air than that delivered by nasal cannulas or simple face masks. Therefore, positive-pressure ventilation provides a constant and controllable FIO<sub>2</sub>. The physiology of noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation is&#x000a0;similar to&#x000a0;that of invasive ventilation (ie, endotracheal tube); however, it requires higher pressures to overcome upper airway resistance, which may result in a mask-face interface leak, reducing effective ventilation and oxygenation, and increasing risk for aspiration. Noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation has specific indications, such as cardiogenic pulmonary edema or acute exacerbation of COPD. However, it is not a first-line treatment for de novo acute hypoxemic respiratory failure and has several contraindications, including severe hypoxemic respiratory failure.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>According to the alveolar gas equation, PAO<sub>2</sub> depends on atmospheric pressure, FIO<sub>2</sub>, and PACO<sub>2</sub>. However, despite positive pressure ventilation, atmospheric pressure&#x000a0;remains&#x000a0;constant at sea level&#x000a0;(760 mmHg). If PEEP is applied, it increases the effective atmospheric pressure; however, PEEP is expressed in cm H<sub>2</sub>O rather than mmHg. Therefore, since&#x000a0;1 cm H<sub>2</sub>O =&#x000a0;0.735 mmHg, a PEEP of 10 adds only&#x000a0;7.4 mmHg to atmospheric pressure in the alveolar gas equation and makes a minimal contribution to PAO<sub>2</sub>. However, PEEP&#x000a0;does improve&#x000a0;oxygenation, the A-a gradient, and the P/F ratio by&#x000a0;reducing atelectasis and V/Q mismatch.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Chronic Respiratory Failure&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The&#x000a0;American Thoracic Society guidelines&#x000a0;on home oxygen therapy for adults with chronic lung disease recommend prescribing long-term oxygen therapy for at least 15 h/d in&#x000a0;the following&#x000a0;cases:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients with COPD and severe&#x000a0;resting&#x000a0;hypoxemia breathing room air:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>PaO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003c;&#x000a0;55 mmHg or SpO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003c; 88%&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>PaO<sub>2</sub> of 56&#x000a0;to 59 mm&#x000a0;Hg or SpO<sub>2</sub> of 89%,&#x000a0;with edema, hematocrit &#x0003e;55%, or P pulmonale on an electrocardiogram.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients with&#x000a0;COPD and severe exertional hypoxemia on&#x000a0;room air</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Adults with interstitial lung disease who have severe chronic resting room air hypoxemia&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Adults with interstitial lung disease who have severe exertional room air hypoxemia</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>PO<sub>2</sub> Targets&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>In<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>a single-center, open-label, randomized trial,&#x000a0;Massimo<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>et al reported that ICU patients receiving conservative supplemental oxygen (goal&#x000a0;PaO<sub>2</sub> 70-100 mmHg or&#x000a0;SpO<sub>2</sub> 94%-98%) had lower ICU mortality compared to those receiving conventional oxygen therapy (goal&#x000a0;PaO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003c;150 mmHg&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;SpO<sub>2</sub> 97%-100%).&#x000a0;However, the results were considered preliminary because the trial was&#x000a0;terminated&#x000a0;early.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A 2016&#x000a0;meta-analysis involving 25&#x000a0;randomized&#x000a0;controlled trials&#x000a0;enrolled&#x000a0;16,037 patients&#x000a0;with sepsis, critical illness, stroke, trauma, myocardial infarction, or cardiac arrest, and patients who had emergency surgery. These randomized controlled trials compared outcomes&#x000a0;among&#x000a0;patients receiving liberal versus conservative oxygen therapy.&#x000a0;The goal&#x000a0;SPO<sub>2</sub> was 94% to 99% in the liberal group versus 94% to 96% in the conservative group. This study showed that liberal oxygen therapy&#x000a0;increases mortality without improving patient outcomes, and that SPO<sub>2 </sub>goals greater than 94% to 96%&#x000a0;are&#x000a0;associated with unfavorable outcomes.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>However, 2 large trials in 2020 investigating outcomes of liberal versus conservative oxygen therapy in mechanically ventilated patients and patients with ARDS&#x000a0;failed to&#x000a0;show a reduction in mechanical ventilation days or improved survival.&#x000a0;Specifically, in patients with ARDS,&#x000a0;Barrot et al reported that a goal&#x000a0;PaO<sub>2</sub>&#x000a0;of 55&#x000a0;to 70 mmHg&#x000a0;in the conservative group, compared to a goal PaO<sub>2</sub> of 90 to&#x000a0;105 mmHg, did not improve survival.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>In 2022, a cluster-randomized, cluster-cross-over trial was conducted in critically ill patients receiving mechanical ventilation that compared outcomes among those receiving a low SpO2 target (goal 88%-92%), an intermediate SpO2 target (goal 92%-96%), and a higher SpO<sub>2</sub> target (goal 96%-100%). When SPO<sub>2</sub> was unavailable or inaccurate, oxygen therapy was adjusted to a target PaO<sub>2</sub> of&#x000a0;60 mmHg in the low-target group,&#x000a0;70 mmHg in the intermediate-target group, and&#x000a0;110 mmHg in the higher-target group. This study showed that the number&#x000a0;of ventilator-free days did not differ among the low-, intermediate-, and high-oxygen&#x000a0;therapy groups (see <bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Definitions of Conservative and Liberal O<sub>2&#x000a0;</sub>Administration and Outcomes of Major Randomized Trials).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Oxygen Toxicity</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hyperoxia, defined as the delivery of a supratherapeutic amount of oxygen exceeding the body's natural arterial levels of the gas, has been identified as a risk factor for increased mortality in critically ill patients. Oxygen toxicity&#x000a0;arises primarily from oxygen's&#x000a0;unpaired electrons, which generate reactive oxygen species that damage cells. Reactive oxygen species can damage the lungs directly&#x000a0;as well as systemic organs.</p>
        <p>In both the operating room and the critical care environment, oxygen toxicity is&#x000a0;generally caused&#x000a0;by supplemental oxygen exposure, typically at FIO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003e; 0.50.&#x000a0;Oxygen toxicity is associated with a variety of abnormalities, which in turn can have a detrimental effect on end-organ systems (especially the central nervous system), including absorption atelectasis, hypercapnia, diffuse alveolar damage,&#x000a0;lung tissue edema, cerebral and myocardial reperfusion injury, as well as triggering cellular damage via inflammatory cascades originating from the vascular endothelial capillary beds.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Notably, high PAO<sub>2</sub> with supplemental oxygen results in low alveolar nitrogen partial pressure (PAN<sub>2</sub>), alveolar collapse, and atelectasis in areas of low V/Q. This process also counteracts hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, resulting in reduced V/Q, increased physiologic dead space, and hypercapnia<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>(see<bold> Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Oxygen Partial Pressure Limits).&#x000a0;Prolonged oxygen toxicity may ultimately lead to cellular death.</p>
        <p>In many critically ill states, hypoxia often presents a very grave risk of irreversible damage to various organ systems, especially the brain and central nervous system. Traditional logic often dictated that a larger-than-needed amount of supplemental oxygen be delivered to raise the partial pressure of oxygen in the blood. This approach was often used to provide adequate oxygen for organ and tissue metabolism despite potentially poor perfusion in conditions such as sepsis, acute respiratory distress syndrome, and cardiogenic shock. As clinical practices have evolved with increasing evidence, the detrimental effects of&#x000a0;hyperoxygenation&#x02014;especially over prolonged periods, such as during long-term ICU ventilation or extended surgical procedures&#x02014;have become increasingly recognized. Divers, premature infants, and hyperbaric therapy patients are also at a heightened risk for oxygen toxicity.</p>
        <p>Regarding&#x000a0;reperfusion injury, a large multicenter retrospective study by Kilgannon et al found that a PaO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003e; 300 was associated with increased in-hospital death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Another study by Page et al reported that increased&#x000a0;hyperoxic&#x000a0;PaO<sub>2</sub> levels correlated with increased mortality in patients who were intubated in the emergency department.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Furthermore, Brenner et al reported increased mortality and lower Glasgow coma score in traumatic brain injury patients exposed to PaO<sub>2</sub> levels &#x0003e;&#x000a0;200 mmHg compared to normal PaO<sub>2</sub> levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;In patients with stroke or myocardial infarction, multiple studies have also&#x000a0;demonstrated&#x000a0;no mortality or functional outcome benefit from providing supplemental oxygen to patients who are not hypoxemic.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In ventilated patients, monitoring of both CO<sub>2</sub>&#x000a0;and O<sub>2</sub>&#x000a0;levels&#x02014;especially in arterial blood&#x02014;is often warranted to assess for supratherapeutic partial pressures of either gas. As the delivered oxygen concentration rises above the atmospheric concentration of 21%, the risk of abnormally elevated dissolved oxygen partial pressures in blood increases directly. Judicious use of supplemental oxygen and restriction of its routine use in patients who do not need it has become a teaching point in many medical institutions. Oxygen therapy is now often taught that its use should be viewed and treated as a medication rather than a routine administration. Public perception, as well as many patients' expectations, often associate oxygen administration with receiving tangible medical care or a standard treatment during hospitalization. Therefore, educating all levels of healthcare providers and patients remains central to effective oxygen stewardship.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Other Areas of Concern:</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Air Travel</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients with underlying lung disease, such as severe COPD and pulmonary fibrosis, are more vulnerable to air travel, even when they do not meet criteria for long-term oxygen therapy. The Aerospace Medical Association, Aviation Safety Committee, Civil Aviation Subcommittee, and other regulatory authorities limit commercial airlines' maximum&#x000a0;cabin altitude to 8000 ft (2438 meters), at which cabin pressure is no lower than 565 mmHg.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;In healthy individuals, a cabin pressure of&#x000a0;565 mmHg corresponds to an SpO<sub>2</sub> greater than 90%. Hence, patients with chronic lung disease have lower baseline resting PaO<sub>2</sub> levels, with corresponding SpO<sub>2</sub> values on the steep&#x000a0;portion&#x000a0;of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve, placing them at risk for severe desaturation from reduced atmospheric or cabin pressure. The following example illustrates the estimated PAO<sub>2&#x000a0;</sub>in a healthy 60-year-old individual with a normal PaCO<sub>2</sub> and respiratory quotient (RQ) at a cruising altitude of 2438 m:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PAO<sub>2</sub> = 0.21 &#x000d7; (565 &#x02013; 47) &#x02013; 40 / 0.8 &#x02248;&#x000a0;59 mmHg</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The 2022 British Thoracic Society Clinical Statement on air travel for passengers with respiratory disease by Coker R et al recommends that patients with COPD and a resting SpO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003c; 95% on room air who are not at risk of hypercapnia receive in-flight oxygen at 2 L/min. Patients&#x000a0;who are not at risk of hypercapnia should undergo a hypoxic challenge test (HCT). Additionally, the British Thoracic Society recommends consideration of in-flight oxygen or HCT if SpO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003c; 84% during the 6-minute walk test.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>The HCT consists of the patient breathing 15% (FIO<sub>2</sub> 0.15) oxygen for 20 minutes. Based on the extent of hypoxemia, blood gas analysis, and recovery, the patient's pulmonologist prescribes in-flight oxygen as indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Diving Injuries&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Decompression sickness and nitrogen narcosis are well-known injuries associated with scuba diving. Recreational divers typically use open-circuit tanks filled with air&#x02014;approximately 21% oxygen and 79% nitrogen&#x02014;pressurized to around 3000 PSI (approximately 204 atm). The regulator reduces this high pressure to 1 atm for breathing. As the diver descends, water pressure increases by 1 atm for every 10 meters, raising the partial pressures of gases in the alveoli. Higher partial pressures of nitrogen increase its solubility in blood and tissues, potentially leading to nitrogen narcosis. Similarly, elevated partial pressures of oxygen increase the risk of oxygen toxicity, though such toxicity from nitrogen or oxygen is rare at depths greater than 100 ft (approximately&#x000a0;30.5 meters).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>Decompression illness, known as the bends, occurs when a diver ascends too rapidly. This rapid ascent causes a large pressure difference between the nitrogen dissolved in the blood and tissues and the ambient water pressure, as described by Henry law. The sudden pressure shift allows nitrogen gas to come out of solution more rapidly than it can be eliminated by the lungs, leading to tissue injury and air embolisms.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A shallow-water blackout is especially relevant to the partial pressure of oxygen and free diving. When a free diver holds their breath and descends, lung volumes decrease, and alveolar gas pressures increase. Although higher partial pressures of oxygen increase arterial blood oxygen levels and oxygen for aerobic metabolism, oxygen consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> production continue. The brain&#x000a0;stem's&#x000a0;bulbar region is&#x000a0;very sensitive&#x000a0;to pH and PaCO<sub>2</sub> changes, which are the essential triggers for the respiratory drive, more so than PaO<sub>2</sub>. This physiologic response serves as a warning to the diver, stimulating air hunger and prompting an ascent before hypoxia occurs. However, hyperventilating to decrease PaCO<sub>2</sub> and&#x000a0;suppress&#x000a0;the respiratory drive increases the risk of hypoxia, especially near the surface. During ascent, PAO<sub>2</sub> decreases as water pressure decreases, potentially causing divers to black out at the surface, a phenomenon known as shallow-water blackout.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Carbon Monoxide Poisoning&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Carbon monoxide binds to hemoglobin with an affinity approximately 250 times greater than that of O<sub>2</sub>, forming carboxyhemoglobin (CO-Hb), resulting in impaired O<sub>2</sub> delivery and tissue hypoxia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;Standard pulse oximetry does not differentiate between&#x000a0;carboxyhemoglobin and oxyhemoglobin. Therefore, the diagnosis is based on a history of cardiac output exposure (ie, a house fire) and elevated carboxyhemoglobin levels on CO-oximetry. The half-life of CO-Hb&#x000a0;is less than 4 hours. Importantly, numerous studies have shown that this time interval can be reduced to as little as 45 minutes with 100% supplemental oxygen (PaO<sub>2</sub> &#x02248; 650 mmHg).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyperbaric Oxygen</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Hyperbaric oxygen (HBO) therapy involves breathing 100% oxygen (FIO<sub>2</sub> 1.0) at a pressure greater than atmospheric pressure (1&#x000a0;atm&#x000a0;&#x02248; 1 ATA &#x02248;&#x000a0;760 mmHg), typically ranging from 1 to 3 atmospheres absolute (ATA). As discussed above, 0.3 mL/dL&#x000a0;of O<sub>2</sub> is dissolved in blood at 1 ATA. This dissolved oxygen contributes minimally to blood oxygen content, which is primarily&#x000a0;determined&#x000a0;by hemoglobin concentration and hemoglobin oxygen saturation.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The risk of oxygen toxicity also applies to oxygen delivered at hyperbaric pressures, as the generation of oxygen-free radicals in a hyper-oxygenated state exceeds the body's innate ability to clear reactive oxygen species. In patients receiving HBO at an FIO<sub>2</sub> of 1.0 and a pressure of 3 ATA (approximately 2280 mmHg), the amount of dissolved oxygen in blood increases&#x000a0;substantially to&#x000a0;approximately 6 mL/dL.&#x000a0;This&#x000a0;level of&#x000a0;dissolved oxygen is sufficient to support tissue oxygen extraction but exposes patients to&#x000a0;the risk of&#x000a0;oxygen toxicity.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In 1878, Paul Bert described oxygen toxicity thresholds, demonstrating that pulmonary toxicity typically occurs with prolonged exposure to PO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003e; 0.5 ATA (approximately&#x000a0;380 mmHg) and that neurologic toxicity occurs with short-term exposure to PO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003e; 3 ATA (approximately 2280 mmHg).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Neurologic symptoms included muscle fasciculation, confusion, vertigo, and seizures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;The central nervous system consequences of oxygen toxicity described by the Bert Effect were further characterized by Camporesi to be dose-dependent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;These dose-dependent thresholds are important in dive medicine (eg, scuba diving and free-diving accidents) and in HBO therapy. In fact, the US Navy recommends a PO<sub>2</sub> &#x0003c; 1.4 ATA as the upper limit for recreational open-circuit scuba tank diving (see <bold>Image.</bold> Oxygen Partial Pressure Limits).[<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.navsea.navy.mil/Portals/103/Documents/SUPSALV/Diving/US%20DIVING%20MANUAL_REV7.pdf">U.S. Navy Diving Manual. 2016</ext-link>] Additionally, when studying the Bert Effect, Lorraine Smith first described the insidious damage of HBO delivery at supraphysiologic partial pressures that results in the direct damage of reactive oxygen species on pulmonary tissues after 4 days of exposure to 73% oxygen, now known as the Smith Effect.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>Despite these risks, there are some well-established benefits to hyperoxia, such as the provision of HBO to individuals who have been victims of diving accidents (eg, decompression sickness), carbon monoxide poisoning, severe anemia, peripheral vascular disease, and wound healing (see <bold>Image.</bold> Upper Left: Deck Decompression Chamber Used in Commercial Diving. Upper Right: Outer Lock of Deck Decompression Chamber Looking into the Inner Lock, Crossover Valve Pictured. Lower: Inner Lock of Deck Decompression Chamber Looking Outward).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26710.r36">[36]</xref><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Reversal of nitrogen toxicosis, mitigation of the effects of CO, and acceleration of wound healing are all benefits that warrant HBO therapy. Ongoing research continues to focus on minimizing the risks associated with HBO treatment, particularly as new applications emerge, such as the use of hyperbaric methods in the management of traumatic brain injury.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26710.s5" sec-type="Other Issues">
        <title>Other Issues</title>
        <p>In conclusion, oxygen is essential to the successful adaptation and proliferation of virtually all life. The&#x000a0;2 identical oxygen molecules chemically connected by a dynamic double bond serve as the fuel source for aerobic metabolism within all mammals. This diatomic structure provides oxygen with the inherent stability necessary for cellular and membrane transport within the mitochondrial apparatus. Although perhaps not the most numerically significant gas in Earth's atmosphere, oxygen surely remains the most important for supporting life. The delicate interplay between the 21% oxygen gas in Earth's atmosphere,&#x000a0;the 78% nitrogen, and trace amounts of carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) is essential for sustaining all life dependent on aerobic metabolism. Only bacteria capable of anaerobic metabolism, along with viruses, can exist in the absence of the life-giving oxygen molecule.&#x000a0;At&#x000a0;sea level, atmospheric pressure remains constant at 760 mmHg.&#x000a0;The composition of this pressure&#x000a0;includes the partial pressures of nitrogen at 593 mmHg, oxygen at 160 mmHg, and a much smaller contribution from&#x000a0;water vapor.&#x000a0;Although nitrogen (N<sub>2</sub>) is considered physiologically&#x000a0;inert, it too contributes substantially to&#x000a0;atmospheric and alveolar stability (see I<bold>mages&#x000a0;</bold>Components of Air and Partial Pressure at STPD (1 atm, 0 &#x000b0;c, 0% Humidity) and&#x000a0;Partial Pressures of Respiratory Gases in Air Samples and Blood).</p>
        <p>This review article systematically examines the importance of oxygen and provides an effective interpretation of the partial pressure of oxygen within the context of human respiratory physiology. This understanding is essential for clinicians practicing in Anesthesiology, Critical Care Medicine (CCM), and Pulmonary Medicine.&#x000a0;Applying these concepts provides a review of the fundamental gas laws and the principles governing gas exchange, oxygen diffusion, and cellular&#x000a0;or membrane transport. This scientific review attempted to explain in great detail the physiologic mechanisms and clinical significance of the partial pressure of oxygen. In particular, the pivotal role of the partial pressure of oxygen in determining arterial and venous hemoglobin saturation within RBCs is emphasized. Finally, the fundamental importance of monitoring arterial saturation&#x02014;whether through ABG analysis or continuous pulse oximetry (SpO2)&#x02014;is underscored.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26710.s6" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>All members of the interprofessional healthcare team, particularly those involved in the care of patients with respiratory or circulatory conditions that impair oxygen delivery, should possess a clear understanding of the concept and physiological implications of the partial pressure of oxygen. This team includes clinicians, clinical specialists, nurses, respiratory and pulmonary therapists, critical care staff, and other allied healthcare professionals. A sound knowledge of PO2 is essential for interpreting ABG results, assessing the adequacy of oxygenation, and recognizing disturbances in gas exchange, ventilation, perfusion, or tissue oxygen delivery.</p>
        <p>An understanding of PO<sub>2</sub> is essential for clinical decision-making and patient management. Accurate interpretation of PO<sub>2&#x000a0;</sub>values aids in diagnosing hypoxemia and respiratory failure and helps guide oxygen therapy, ventilatory support, and other therapeutic interventions. When considered alongside other clinical parameters, it supports timely treatment decisions and contributes to improved patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26710.s7">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
